    "---\n",
    "# Module 3 — Loading the Model\n",
    "\n",
    "We are now going to take Qwen3-0.6B out of its box, load its 600 million parameters into VRAM, build the tokenizer pipeline, explore sampling strategies, and ultimately build a professional streaming inference engine."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Loading the Model Weights (Lesson 3.1)\n",
    "\n",
    "The `.safetensors` file sitting on Hugging Face's servers contains 600 million floating-point numbers. We need to:\n",
    "1. Download the file.\n",
    "2. Build an empty PyTorch skeleton from `config.json`.\n",
    "3. Fill the skeleton with the actual weights (\"hydration\").\n",
    "4. Transfer everything into the T4 GPU's VRAM.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use `AutoModelForCausalLM` (not `AutoModel`) because we need the **Language Modeling Head** — the final layer that outputs logits for next-token prediction. We load in FP16 to halve the VRAM footprint from ~2.4 GB to ~1.2 GB."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import torch\n",
    "from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\n",
    "\n",
    "MODEL_ID = \"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\"\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"📥 Downloading and loading {MODEL_ID}...\")\n",
    "\n",
    "model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n",
    "    MODEL_ID,\n",
    "    torch_dtype=torch.float16,   # Half precision: 2 bytes per param instead of 4\n",
    "    device_map=\"auto\"            # accelerate places weights on the GPU automatically\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "vram_allocated = torch.cuda.memory_allocated() / (1024 ** 3)\n",
    "print(f\"✅ Model loaded!\")\n",
    "print(f\"GPU VRAM Allocated: {vram_allocated:.2f} GB\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Inspecting the Architecture\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's look at the actual PyTorch object. You will see the `embed_tokens` embedding layer, 28 `Qwen2DecoderLayer` blocks (each containing `q_proj`, `k_proj`, `v_proj` attention projections and SwiGLU FFN), and the `lm_head` at the bottom."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Print the full architecture tree\n",
    "print(model)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Loading the Tokenizer (Lesson 3.2)\n",
    "\n",
    "The model only understands integer tensor IDs. The tokenizer translates human text into token IDs using Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE). Qwen3 has a vocabulary of **151,646 unique tokens**.\n",
    "\n",
    "The tokenizer also handles **ChatML formatting** — wrapping messages with `<|im_start|>` and `<|im_end|>` special tokens that teach the model who is speaking."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL_ID)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Fix padding if not set (required for batch generation)\n",
    "if tokenizer.pad_token_id is None:\n",
    "    tokenizer.pad_token_id = tokenizer.eos_token_id\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Tokenizer loaded!\")\n",
    "print(f\"Vocabulary Size:    {tokenizer.vocab_size}\")\n",
    "print(f\"EOS Token:          {tokenizer.eos_token} (ID: {tokenizer.eos_token_id})\")\n",
    "print(f\"Pad Token:          {tokenizer.pad_token} (ID: {tokenizer.pad_token_id})\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Exploring Tokenization\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's see how the tokenizer splits text into subword tokens. Notice how common words stay intact, while rarer words get split into subword fragments."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "test_text = \"Yo Sahur, explain attention mechanisms!\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Encode: text → token IDs\n",
    "token_ids = tokenizer.encode(test_text)\n",
    "print(f\"Text:      '{test_text}'\")\n",
    "print(f\"Token IDs: {token_ids}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Num Tokens: {len(token_ids)}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show each token individually\n",
    "print(\"\\nToken Breakdown:\")\n",
    "for tid in token_ids:\n",
    "    print(f\"  ID {tid:>6} → {repr(tokenizer.decode([tid]))}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Decode: token IDs → text\n",
    "decoded = tokenizer.decode(token_ids)\n",
    "print(f\"\\nDecoded:   '{decoded}'\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### ChatML Formatting with `apply_chat_template`\n",
    "\n",
    "In real inference, we don't manually tokenize raw strings. We structure the conversation into roles (`system`, `user`, `assistant`) and let the tokenizer wrap them in ChatML. The `add_generation_prompt=True` flag appends `<|im_start|>assistant\\n` to signal the model that it should now generate a response."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "messages = [\n",
    "    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Sahur, a Gen-Z coding assistant.\"},\n",
    "    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"how do I reverse a list in python?\"}\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert to ChatML string (not tokenized yet)\n",
    "chatml_string = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(\n",
    "    messages,\n",
    "    tokenize=False,\n",
    "    add_generation_prompt=True\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"--- Raw ChatML String ---\")\n",
    "print(chatml_string)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. First Generation — The Autoregressive Loop (Lesson 3.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "An LLM generates text **one token at a time**. `model.generate()` automates this loop:\n",
    "1. Forward pass through 28 Transformer layers → outputs 151,646 logits.\n",
    "2. Select the winning token ID.\n",
    "3. Append to the sequence.\n",
    "4. Check stop conditions (`<|im_end|>` or `max_new_tokens`).\n",
    "5. Loop back."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Tokenize the ChatML prompt and move to GPU\n",
    "inputs = tokenizer(chatml_string, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device)\n",
    "prompt_length = inputs[\"input_ids\"].shape[1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Prompt token count: {prompt_length}\")\n",
    "print(f\"input_ids shape:    {inputs['input_ids'].shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"attention_mask shape: {inputs['attention_mask'].shape}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Run the autoregressive loop (greedy decoding, no sampling)\n",
    "with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "    output_ids = model.generate(\n",
    "        **inputs,\n",
    "        max_new_tokens=100,\n",
    "        do_sample=False   # Greedy: always pick the highest probability token\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "# Slice off the prompt tokens to get ONLY the generated response\n",
    "response_ids = output_ids[0][prompt_length:]\n",
    "response_text = tokenizer.decode(response_ids, skip_special_tokens=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"\\nGenerated {len(response_ids)} new tokens.\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\n🤖 Sahur: {response_text}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 9. Sampling Strategies (Lessons 3.4–3.7)\n",
    "\n",
    "Greedy decoding (always picking the highest-probability token) produces deterministic but often boring, repetitive text. Real inference uses **sampling** — randomly selecting from the probability distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Temperature** (3.5): Controls randomness. Low (0.2) = focused/deterministic. High (1.5) = creative/chaotic.\n",
    "- **Top-K** (3.6): Only sample from the K highest-probability tokens. Cuts off the long tail.\n",
    "- **Top-P / Nucleus** (3.7): Only sample from tokens whose cumulative probability reaches P. Adapts dynamically."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def generate_with_params(prompt_text, temperature=0.8, top_k=50, top_p=0.9, max_tokens=100):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Generate a response with specific sampling parameters.\"\"\"\n",
    "    msgs = [\n",
    "        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Sahur, a Gen-Z coding assistant.\"},\n",
    "        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt_text}\n",
    "    ]\n",
    "    formatted = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(msgs, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n",
    "    inp = tokenizer(formatted, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device)\n",
    "    plen = inp[\"input_ids\"].shape[1]\n",
    "\n",
    "    with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "        out = model.generate(\n",
    "            **inp,\n",
    "            max_new_tokens=max_tokens,\n",
    "            do_sample=True,\n",
    "            temperature=temperature,\n",
    "            top_k=top_k,\n",
    "            top_p=top_p,\n",
    "            repetition_penalty=1.15,\n",
    "            pad_token_id=tokenizer.pad_token_id\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "    return tokenizer.decode(out[0][plen:], skip_special_tokens=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Experiment: Temperature Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "Run the same prompt at different temperatures to see how randomness affects the output."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "prompt = \"write a one-liner joke about Python programming\"\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"--- Temperature 0.2 (Very Focused) ---\")\n",
    "print(generate_with_params(prompt, temperature=0.2))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n--- Temperature 0.8 (Balanced) ---\")\n",
    "print(generate_with_params(prompt, temperature=0.8))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n--- Temperature 1.5 (Very Creative) ---\")\n",
    "print(generate_with_params(prompt, temperature=1.5))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 10. Streaming Inference Engine (Lessons 3.8–3.9)\n",
    "\n",
    "Standard `model.generate()` is a blocking function — you wait seconds for the full output. `TextStreamer` intercepts the autoregressive loop and prints each token the instant it's generated, just like ChatGPT.\n",
    "\n",
    "We also enable `use_cache=True` for the **KV Cache** (Lesson 3.8), which prevents the model from recomputing attention for all previous tokens on every step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from transformers import TextStreamer\n",
    "\n",
    "class SahurInferenceEngine:\n",
    "    def __init__(self, model, tokenizer):\n",
    "        self.model = model\n",
    "        self.tokenizer = tokenizer\n",
    "\n",
    "        # skip_prompt=True: Don't echo the user's question back\n",
    "        self.streamer = TextStreamer(tokenizer, skip_prompt=True, skip_special_tokens=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Golden Sampling Config (from Lessons 3.4-3.7)\n",
    "        self.gen_config = {\n",
    "            \"max_new_tokens\": 200,\n",
    "            \"do_sample\": True,\n",
    "            \"temperature\": 0.8,\n",
    "            \"top_k\": 50,\n",
    "            \"top_p\": 0.90,\n",
    "            \"repetition_penalty\": 1.15,\n",
    "            \"pad_token_id\": tokenizer.pad_token_id,\n",
    "            \"eos_token_id\": tokenizer.eos_token_id,\n",
    "            \"use_cache\": True  # KV Cache ON (Lesson 3.8)\n",
    "        }\n",
    "        print(\"✅ SahurInferenceEngine ready.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    def chat(self, user_message):\n",
    "        messages = [\n",
    "            {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Sahur, a hyper-responsive Gen Z AI assistant.\"},\n",
    "            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_message}\n",
    "        ]\n",
    "        prompt = self.tokenizer.apply_chat_template(\n",
    "            messages, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True\n",
    "        )\n",
    "        inputs = self.tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(self.model.device)\n",
    "\n",
    "        print(\"\\n🤖 Sahur: \", end=\"\")\n",
    "        with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "            self.model.generate(**inputs, streamer=self.streamer, **self.gen_config)\n",
    "        print(\"\\n\" + \"─\"*50)\n",
    "\n",
    "engine = SahurInferenceEngine(model, tokenizer)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Interactive Chat Loop\n",
    "\n",
    "This cell starts a continuous chat session. Type your messages and watch the tokens stream in real-time. Type `exit` to end."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"💬 Chat session started! (Type 'exit' to end)\")\n",
    "print(\"─\"*50)\n",
    "\n",
    "while True:\n",
    "    try:\n",
    "        user_input = input(\"🧑 You: \")\n",
    "        if user_input.lower() in ['exit', 'quit']:\n",
    "            print(\"👋 Peace out!\")\n",
    "            break\n",
    "        if not user_input.strip():\n",
    "            continue\n",
    "        engine.chat(user_input)\n",
    "    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n",
    "        print(\"\\n👋 Session interrupted.\")\n",
    "        break"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# Module 4 — Exploring the Model\n",
    "\n",
    "We've loaded the model and run inference. But so far, we've been treating Qwen3 as a black box: prompt in → text out.\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we're going to **open the box**. We will inspect the model's internal representations (hidden states), visualize where it focuses attention, examine the raw decision scores (logits), convert them to probabilities, measure uncertainty with entropy, and build a step-by-step token prediction inspector."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 11. Hidden States — The Model's Internal Representations (Lesson 4.1)\n",
    "\n",
    "As a prompt passes through each of Qwen3's 28 Transformer layers, the token representations evolve. The first layer captures surface-level features (grammar, word identity). The deeper layers capture abstract semantic meaning. We can extract these hidden states by passing `output_hidden_states=True`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Set model to evaluation mode (disables dropout)\n",
    "model.eval()\n",
    "\n",
    "prompt = \"Artificial Intelligence will\"\n",
    "inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device)\n",
    "\n",
    "with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "    outputs = model(\n",
    "        **inputs,\n",
    "        output_hidden_states=True  # This unlocks hidden state extraction!\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "hidden_states = outputs.hidden_states  # Tuple of tensors, one per layer\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Number of hidden state layers: {len(hidden_states)} (embedding + 28 decoder layers)\")\n",
    "print(f\"Shape of each layer: {hidden_states[0].shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → [batch_size, sequence_length, hidden_dim]\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → [1, {hidden_states[0].shape[1]} tokens, {hidden_states[0].shape[2]} dimensions]\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Comparing Early vs Deep Layers\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's compare the representations of the word \"Intelligence\" at Layer 1 (surface) vs Layer 28 (deepest). We use cosine similarity to measure how much the representation changed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import torch.nn.functional as F\n",
    "\n",
    "# Find the token index for \"Intelligence\" in our prompt\n",
    "token_ids_list = tokenizer.encode(prompt)\n",
    "print(\"Tokens:\", [tokenizer.decode([t]) for t in token_ids_list])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Pick the token index for the first meaningful word (index may vary)\n",
    "token_idx = 1  # Adjust if needed based on the token breakdown above\n",
    "\n",
    "# Extract the hidden state for that token at Layer 1 and the last layer\n",
    "layer_1_vec = hidden_states[1][0, token_idx, :]   # Layer 1\n",
    "layer_last_vec = hidden_states[-1][0, token_idx, :] # Last layer\n",
    "\n",
    "# Cosine similarity: 1.0 = identical, 0.0 = unrelated\n",
    "similarity = F.cosine_similarity(\n",
    "    layer_1_vec.unsqueeze(0).float(),\n",
    "    layer_last_vec.unsqueeze(0).float()\n",
    ").item()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"\\nCosine similarity between Layer 1 and Layer {len(hidden_states)-1}: {similarity:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(\"→ Lower similarity = the model significantly transformed the representation.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 12. Attention Maps — Where the Model Looks (Lesson 4.2)\n",
    "\n",
    "Attention weights tell us which tokens the model focused on when processing each position. We extract them by passing `output_attentions=True`. The result is a tensor of shape `[batch, heads, seq_len, seq_len]` — a square matrix showing how much each token \"attended to\" every other token."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "    attn_outputs = model(\n",
    "        **inputs,\n",
    "        output_attentions=True\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "attentions = attn_outputs.attentions  # Tuple of tensors, one per layer\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Number of attention layers: {len(attentions)}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Shape of Layer 0 attention: {attentions[0].shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → [batch, num_heads, seq_len, seq_len]\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Visualizing Attention (Layer 0, Head 0)\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's look at the attention pattern from the very first layer. Each row shows how much attention a token paid to every preceding token."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Extract Layer 0, Head 0 attention matrix\n",
    "attn_matrix = attentions[0][0, 0].float().cpu()  # [seq_len, seq_len]\n",
    "tokens = [tokenizer.decode([t]) for t in token_ids_list]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Attention Map for Layer 0, Head 0:\")\n",
    "print(f\"Tokens: {tokens}\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Print a simple text-based heatmap\n",
    "header = \"         \" + \" \".join(f\"{t:>8}\" for t in tokens)\n",
    "print(header)\n",
    "for i, row_token in enumerate(tokens):\n",
    "    row_values = \" \".join(f\"{attn_matrix[i, j]:.4f}\" if j <= i else \"   ---  \" for j in range(len(tokens)))\n",
    "    print(f\"{row_token:>8} {row_values}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n→ Higher values = stronger attention between token pairs.\")\n",
    "print(\"→ '---' = causal mask (can't attend to future tokens).\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 13. Logits — The Raw Decision Scores (Lesson 4.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "After the prompt passes through all 28 layers, the LM Head outputs a vector of **151,646 raw scores** (one for every token in the vocabulary). These are the **logits** — the model's unnormalized opinion about what should come next. They are NOT probabilities yet."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# We already have the outputs from the forward pass\n",
    "logits = attn_outputs.logits\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Logits tensor shape: {logits.shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → [batch=1, seq_len={logits.shape[1]}, vocab_size={logits.shape[2]}]\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Extract the logits for the LAST token position (the prediction for the next word)\n",
    "next_token_logits = logits[0, -1, :]  # Shape: [151646]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"\\nPrediction logits shape: {next_token_logits.shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Min logit: {next_token_logits.min().item():.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Max logit: {next_token_logits.max().item():.2f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Top 10 raw logit scores\n",
    "top_values, top_indices = torch.topk(next_token_logits, k=10)\n",
    "print(f\"\\nTop 10 Logit Scores (raw, unnormalized):\")\n",
    "for i in range(10):\n",
    "    tok = tokenizer.decode([top_indices[i].item()])\n",
    "    print(f\"  {repr(tok):<15} logit: {top_values[i].item():.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 14. Probabilities & Entropy (Lesson 4.4)\n",
    "\n",
    "Logits are hard to interpret because they have no fixed scale. **Softmax** converts them into a proper probability distribution that sums to 1.0. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Entropy** then tells us how *uncertain* the model is. Low entropy = one token dominates (model is confident). High entropy = many tokens are competitive (model is uncertain)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Convert logits to probabilities using Softmax\n",
    "probs = torch.softmax(next_token_logits.float(), dim=-1)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Probability sum: {probs.sum().item():.6f} (should be ≈ 1.0)\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Top 10 as actual percentages\n",
    "top_probs, top_prob_indices = torch.topk(probs, k=10)\n",
    "print(f\"\\nTop 10 Predictions (Probability):\")\n",
    "for i in range(10):\n",
    "    tok = tokenizer.decode([top_prob_indices[i].item()])\n",
    "    print(f\"  {repr(tok):<15} {top_probs[i].item()*100:6.2f}%\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Calculate Entropy\n",
    "entropy = -torch.sum(probs * torch.log(probs + 1e-12)).item()\n",
    "print(f\"\\n🧠 Entropy: {entropy:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(\"→ Low entropy = confident. High entropy = uncertain.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 15. Token Prediction Inspector (Lesson 4.5)\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we combine everything into a single tool that lets us watch the model think **one token at a time**. Instead of using `model.generate()`, we manually run the forward pass, inspect the logits, compute probabilities, measure entropy, select the token, and append it — for every step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def run_token_inspector(prompt, max_new_tokens=5, top_k=5):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Watch the model predict tokens one at a time.\"\"\"\n",
    "    print(f\"\\n{'='*55}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"🔬 TOKEN PREDICTION INSPECTOR\")\n",
    "    print(f\"Prompt: '{prompt}'\")\n",
    "    print(f\"{'='*55}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=\"pt\").to(model.device)\n",
    "    generated = inputs[\"input_ids\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "    for step in range(1, max_new_tokens + 1):\n",
    "        # 1. Forward pass\n",
    "        with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "            outputs = model(input_ids=generated)\n",
    "\n",
    "        # 2. Extract last-position logits\n",
    "        next_logits = outputs.logits[:, -1, :]\n",
    "\n",
    "        # 3. Softmax → probabilities\n",
    "        probs = torch.softmax(next_logits.float(), dim=-1)\n",
    "\n",
    "        # 4. Entropy\n",
    "        entropy = -torch.sum(probs * torch.log(probs + 1e-12), dim=-1).item()\n",
    "\n",
    "        # 5. Top-K candidates\n",
    "        values, indices = torch.topk(probs, k=top_k)\n",
    "\n",
    "        print(f\"\\n--- STEP {step} ---\")\n",
    "        print(\"Top Predictions:\")\n",
    "        for i in range(top_k):\n",
    "            tok = tokenizer.decode([indices[0][i].item()])\n",
    "            print(f\"  {i+1}. {repr(tok):<15} {values[0][i].item()*100:6.2f}%\")\n",
    "        print(f\"Entropy: {entropy:.3f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "        # 6. Greedy selection\n",
    "        next_token = torch.argmax(probs, dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n",
    "        chosen = tokenizer.decode(next_token[0])\n",
    "        print(f\"✅ Chosen: {repr(chosen)}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "        # 7. Append and show growing sequence\n",
    "        generated = torch.cat([generated, next_token], dim=-1)\n",
    "        current = tokenizer.decode(generated[0], skip_special_tokens=True)\n",
    "        print(f\"📝 Sequence: {current}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "        # 8. EOS check\n",
    "        if next_token.item() == tokenizer.eos_token_id:\n",
    "            print(\"🛑 EOS token reached.\")\n",
    "            break\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"✅ TokenInspector loaded!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
